"Find out how the size of a telescope’s mirror and the wavelength of the light set the finest detail it can see, and why radio telescopes have to be enormous.",
"<strong>Light spreads.</strong> Light passing through any opening spreads out a little: this is <strong>diffraction</strong>. So even a perfect telescope does not focus a star to a point. It makes a small blurred disk with faint rings around it. The bigger the mirror compared with the wavelength of the light, the smaller the disk.",
"<strong>Two stars.</strong> When two stars are close together in the sky, their disks overlap. If the disks overlap too much, the two stars blur into one. Astronomers use a rule called <strong>Rayleigh’s criterion</strong>: two stars are just seen as two when the brightness halfway between them dips to about {#}% of the peaks, a dip about {#}% deep. The smallest angle between two stars that a telescope can separate is then",
